ZT Bank — open the bank in Firefox as {{ item.value.name }} ({{ item.value.role }})
==================================================================================

There is no password to log in: Firefox proves who you are with your client certificate,
during the TLS handshake. Files in this folder:

  root_ca.crt        the bank's root of trust (public)
  {{ '%-19s' | format(item.value.actor ~ '.p12') }}your key + certificate, encrypted (AES-256)
  p12-password.txt   the password of {{ item.value.actor }}.p12 — delete it once imported
  client.crt / .key  the same identity for curl (stage 8)

Snap Firefox (Ubuntu) can only open files in your home folder, not in hidden folders:
copy this folder to ~/Downloads/zt-{{ item.value.actor }}/ first.

1. Tunnel up:            sudo wg-quick up ./wg0.conf      (the bank is only reachable through it)

2. Trust the bank's root (once per Firefox profile)
   Settings > Privacy & Security > Certificates > View Certificates... > Authorities > Import...
   choose root_ca.crt, tick "Trust this CA to identify websites", OK.
   Before trusting it, check its SHA-256 fingerprint (View) is exactly:
     {{ fingerprint }}

3. Import your identity
   Same window > Your Certificates > Import... > {{ item.value.actor }}.p12 > password from p12-password.txt.
   It appears as "ZT Bank - {{ item.value.name }} ({{ item.value.role }})".

4. Open  {{ item.value.web_url }}
   Firefox asks "User Identification Request": choose that certificate, tick "Remember this
   decision", OK. You are in, as {{ item.value.name }}.

Several identities on one machine (alice and dev): Firefox remembers ONE choice per site, so use
one Firefox profile per identity:   firefox -P      (create "zt-alice", "zt-dev"; do steps 2-3 in each)

Your certificate lives {{ item.value.lifetime }}. When it expires the bank refuses the handshake
(Firefox: "SSL_ERROR_EXPIRED_CERT_ALERT" or "connection reset"): get the new {{ item.value.actor }}.p12 from the
cloud admin (`make client-certs` on the cloud host), delete the old one in Your Certificates, import the new one.
The root and the password stay the same.

